# Optimisation Rules

Rules for compressing self-contained HTML documents to minimise total file size.
All CSS and JS live in a single file — every byte counts.

---

## 1. Naming Convention: Case Separation

Use letter case to clearly separate CSS from JavaScript namespaces.

### CSS (lowercase)

All CSS identifiers use **lowercase single letters**.

- **Classes:** Start from `a` and count up — `.a`, `.b`, `.c`, ...
- **IDs:** Continue after the last class letter if needed.
- **Keyframe names:** Start from `z` and count down — `@keyframes z`, `@keyframes y`, ...
- Classes and keyframes grow toward the middle of the alphabet, leaving a clear gap.

```css
/* Classes: a upward */
.a { position: relative; }
.b { position: absolute; }
.c { background: linear-gradient(...); }

/* Keyframes: z downward */
@keyframes z { from { ... } to { ... } }
@keyframes y { 0% { ... } 100% { ... } }
```

### JavaScript (UPPERCASE)

All JavaScript identifiers use **UPPERCASE single letters**.

- **Constants / globals:** Start from `Z` and count down — `Z`, `Y`, `X`, ...
- **Functions:** Start from `A` and count up — `A`, `B`, `C`, `D`, ...
- **Local variables (params, loop vars, etc.):** Start from `H` and count up — `H`, `I`, `J`, `K`, ...
- Constants and functions grow toward the middle; locals live in between.

```javascript
/* Constants: Z downward */
const Z = '!@#$%^&*()';
const Y = document.querySelectorAll('.f');
const X = someValue;

/* Functions: A upward */
function A(I) { ... }
function B(J, K, P) { ... }
async function D() { ... }

/* Locals / params: H upward */
Y.forEach(H => { ... });           // H = element
function B(J, K, P) {              // J = element, K = delay, P = direction
    const W = X.length;            // W = length
    let L = 0;                     // L = frame counter
    return new Promise(M => { ... }); // M = resolve
}
```

### Allocation Map

```
CSS (lowercase)                    JavaScript (UPPERCASE)

a b c d e f g ...                  ... G F E D C B A       ← functions (A up)
                                   H I J K L M N O P ...   ← locals (H up)
... z y x                          X Y Z                   ← constants (Z down)

classes →    ← keyframes           functions →   locals   ← constants
```

---

## 2. Structural Compression Principles

### 2a. Merge near-identical functions

If two functions share 90%+ of their code and differ only in a condition or
a final value, combine them with a direction/mode parameter.

```javascript
// BAD: Two separate functions with near-identical bodies
function B(J, K) { ... if (N < threshold) ... J[S] = X; ... }
function C(J, K) { ... if (N >= threshold) ... J[S] = A(X); ... }

// GOOD: Single function with a direction flag P
function B(J, K, P) {
    ...
    if ((N < Math.floor((L / 20) * W)) === P) { ... }
    ...
    J[S] = P ? X : A(X);
}
```

### 2b. Extract repeated expressions into helpers

If the same expression appears 2+ times, extract it into a `const` arrow function.

```javascript
// BAD: Repeated 38-char expression in two locations
Z[Math.floor(Math.random() * Z.length)]
Z[Math.floor(Math.random() * Z.length)]

// GOOD: Extracted once
const R = () => Z[Math.floor(Math.random() * Z.length)];
R()  // 3 chars vs 38 chars per call
```

**Rule of thumb:** Extract when `(expression_length × occurrences) > (declaration_length + short_call × occurrences)`.

### 2c. Extract repeated call patterns into helpers

If a Promise pattern, loop pattern, or callback structure is duplicated, wrap it.

```javascript
// BAD: Same stagger pattern repeated twice
await Promise.all([...Y].map((H, N) => B(H, N * 500, 1)));
await Promise.all([...Y].map((H, N) => B(H, N * 500, 0)));

// GOOD: Helper takes the varying part as a parameter
const E = P => Promise.all([...Y].map((H, N) => B(H, N * 500, P)));
await E(1);
await E(0);
```

### 2d. Cache long property names as string keys

When a DOM property like `textContent` (11 chars) is accessed 3+ times,
cache the string and use bracket notation.

```javascript
// BAD: 13 chars per access × 4 uses = 52 chars
J.textContent = O;
J.textContent = X;

// GOOD: 22 char declaration + 4 chars per access × 4 uses = 38 chars
const S = 'textContent';
J[S] = O;
J[S] = X;
```

**Rule of thumb:** Cache when `(property.length × occurrences) > (declaration_length + bracket_access × occurrences)`.

### 2e. Use Map over DOM data attributes

`element.dataset.original` (24 chars) is expensive. A Map is shorter and avoids DOM writes.

```javascript
// BAD: DOM storage
H.dataset.original = X;       // 22 chars
const X = J.dataset.original;  // 26 chars

// GOOD: Map storage
const Q = new Map();           // 18 chars (one-time)
Q.set(H, X);                   // 10 chars
const X = Q.get(J);            // 18 chars
```

### 2f. Prefer arrow functions for single-expression returns

When a function body is a single `return` statement, use an arrow function
to eliminate the `function`, `return`, and brace keywords.

```javascript
// BAD: 89 chars minified
function A(I){return I.split('').map(()=>R()).join('')}

// GOOD: 52 chars minified
const A=I=>I.split('').map(()=>R()).join('')
```

Note: For multi-statement function bodies, arrow functions do not save chars.

### 2g. Inline small constants used in only one function

If a constant is declared and used only 2-3 times within the same scope,
inlining the literal value may be shorter than the declaration + references.

```javascript
// BAD: 11 char declaration + 2 uses of 1 char = 13 chars
const V = 20;
Math.floor((L / V) * W)
if (L > V)

// GOOD: 0 char declaration + 2 uses of 2 chars = 4 chars
Math.floor((L / 20) * W)
if (L > 20)
```

**Rule of thumb:** Inline when `declaration_cost > (literal_length - var_length) × occurrences`.

---

## 3. Commentary Convention

Add comments liberally during development. They will be stripped on minification.

- Document every single-letter identifier with `// X = Description`
- Document function signatures: params, return values, behaviour
- Document non-obvious logic: ternaries, bitwise tricks, condition flips

```javascript
// Z = Character set for scrambled text
const Z = '!@#$%^&*()';

// B = Animate text (reveal or scramble)
// J = DOM element, K = delay ms, P = direction (1=reveal, 0=scramble)
function B(J, K, P) { ... }
```

---

## 4. Minification Checklist

When preparing the final minified version:

1. Strip all comments
2. Strip all unnecessary whitespace and newlines
3. Verify no naming collisions between CSS lowercase and JS uppercase
4. Verify all single-letter names are unique within their scope
5. Test the minified version renders identically to the commented version
